<div class="ephemera-page">

  <section class="ep-hero">
    <div class="ep-kicker">Public services · Discovery support</div>
    <h2>Helping people discover the collection</h2>
    <p>Comics culture extends beyond books. Help students and visitors explore the artists, publishers, bookstores, events, and communities represented in the ephemera collection.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>What is this collection?</h2>
    <p>The Comics &amp; Graphic Novels Ephemera collection brings together smaller printed and physical materials associated with comics and graphic novels. These may include stickers, postcards, posters, flyers, minicomics, buttons, and other related items.</p>
    <p>Many materials come from independent artists, publishers, bookstores, organizations, events, and other participants in comics communities.</p>
    <div class="ep-callout">
      <strong>Think of it as a collection about the culture surrounding comics, as well as the comics themselves.</strong>
      <p>These materials can offer students a different way to encounter visual storytelling, independent publishing, creative communities, and the circulation of ideas.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Why might students be interested?</h2>
    <div class="ep-grid">
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Art &amp; illustration</h3>
        <p>Explore different visual styles, techniques, formats, and approaches to illustration.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Writing &amp; storytelling</h3>
        <p>Encounter the broader world of comics storytelling, independent publications, and creative experimentation.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Publishing &amp; design</h3>
        <p>Discover independent publishers, small presses, bookstores, and the material culture of print.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Community &amp; culture</h3>
        <p>Learn about the artists, organizations, events, and communities that connect people through comics.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Helping someone explore</h2>
    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">01</div>
      <h3>Start with what interests them</h3>
      <p>Ask whether they are interested in a particular artist, publisher, subject, format, event, or type of material.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">02</div>
      <h3>Introduce the physical collection</h3>
      <p>Show them the browsing binders or other designated display materials. Explain that the collection includes smaller pieces that complement the library's comics and graphic novels holdings.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">03</div>
      <h3>Use the inventory to search</h3>
      <p>When someone has a particular interest, use the internal inventory spreadsheet to search the available titles, creators, organizations, and keywords.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">04</div>
      <h3>Locate the physical item</h3>
      <p>Use the item's recorded location to find it in the appropriate binder or flat-file storage area.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="ep-step">
      <div class="ep-number">05</div>
      <h3>Connect the item to other resources</h3>
      <p>When appropriate, help students find related books, graphic novels, publishers, artists, or other library resources.</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Searching the internal inventory</h2>
    <p>The spreadsheet is an internal working inventory rather than a public catalog. It can be especially useful for searches that start with a known creator, publisher, organization, or subject.</p>
    <div class="ep-grid">
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Search by creator</h3>
        <p>Look for an artist, writer, illustrator, publisher, or organization named on the item.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Search by subject</h3>
        <p>Try relevant keywords describing a theme, topic, character, or community.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Search by format</h3>
        <p>Find stickers, postcards, posters, minicomics, buttons/pins, flyers, or Other.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="ep-card">
        <h3>Search by location</h3>
        <p>Use the Location field to retrieve the physical item after identifying its record.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>What staff should know about the inventory</h2>
    <div class="ep-warning">
      <strong>The inventory is an internal staff tool.</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Do not distribute the working spreadsheet as a public resource.</li>
        <li>Do not share donor information or internal notes with visitors.</li>
        <li>Respect any restrictions or access instructions associated with individual materials.</li>
        <li>Refer questions about reproduction, permissions, and unusual materials to the librarian.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>What if someone wants to find more?</h2>
    <p>The ephemera collection is intended to complement the library's broader Comics &amp; Graphic Novels collection. Staff can help visitors connect individual items with related resources, including:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Comics and graphic novels in the library collection.</li>
      <li>Works by an artist, writer, or illustrator represented in the ephemera.</li>
      <li>Other publications from a featured publisher or small press.</li>
      <li>Related resources in art, illustration, writing, publishing, and cultural studies.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>For more complex research questions, refer the visitor to a reference librarian.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ep-section">
    <h2>Help improve discovery</h2>
    <p>Public services staff may notice search terms, subjects, or types of materials that students frequently request but that are not easy to find in the existing inventory.</p>
    <p>Share recurring questions or suggestions with the librarian. These observations can help improve keywords, browsing arrangements, and future discovery tools.</p>
  </section>

